                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#slice_toposes_of_tendues_are_tendues"} Slice toposes of étendues are étendues
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong étendue
            (also ‘etendue’, ‘étendu’, or ‘etendu’; from French ‘étendue’ (fem.)-
            = succeed ")" do
              %em extent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒴
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
            that locally looks like the category of sheaves on a space:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Briefly, the slogan is that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒴
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
              is locally a topological space. (
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere76"}> Lawvere 1976
              , p.129)
          %p
            In some papers,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            are replaced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            in the definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendues"} étendues
            %a{:href => "#Rosenthal"} Rosenthal
            or Definition 1.12 in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#KockMoerdijk"} Kock and Moerdijk
          %p
            Originally defined by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+Grothendieck"} A. Grothendieck
            in one of the famous ‘exercises’ of
            %a{:href => "#SGA4"} SGA4
            (ex. 9.8.2) as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒴
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
            that has a well-supported object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒴
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}/X
            is equivalent to a sheaf topos on a topological space, the definition was generalized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            (1975,1976) by dropping the spatiality of the slice and require only that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒴
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}/X
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+topos"} localic topos
          %p
            Several characterizations of étendues are known and the
            = succeed "-example" do
              %em Ur
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^G
            of group actions, exhibits one in terms of sites rather directly: it has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            where every morphism is monic. Other characterizations involve (local)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
            and yield connections to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliations"} foliations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infinity
              \-étendues
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Carchedi13"} Carchedi 2013
          %p
            Étendues play an important role in Lawvere’s approach to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            and the distinction between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/petit+and+gros+toposes"} petit and gros toposes
            where they provide one of the classes of
            %strong petit
            toposes (generalized spaces). In this context, Lawvere (1989,1991) interprets the cancellative property of the site as enabling an interpretation of étendues as
            = succeed "." do
              %em categories of processes
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒴
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
            is called an
            %em étendue
            if there is an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi 𝒴
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in|\mathcal{Y}|
            such that the unique
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\rightarrow 1
            is epic and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒴
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}/X
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+topos"} localic topos
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %h2#examples Examples
          %blockquote
            %p
              The first example of an étendue seems to have been the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+moduli"} space of moduli
              of algebraic curves, which is prevented from being globally a space due to the action of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+groups"} Galois groups
              within each point. Yes, something vaguely reminiscent of particle spin is going on in such spaces, and the most naked form is that for any group G, the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^G
              is an étendue with only one point! This is easily seen from the observations that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^G/G\cong\mathcal{S}^G
              and that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ↠
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\twoheadrightarrow 1
              where the last two
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Lawvere76"} Lawvere 1976
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"} Sierpinski topos
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝒮
                          %mrow
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mo →
                            %mo ⋅
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\cdot\rightarrow\cdot}
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+space"} Sierpinski space
            %li
              %p
                The topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mo ⇉
                          %mo ⋅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\cdot\rightrightarrows\cdot}
                of
                %em
                  directed
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
                (aka
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quivers"} quivers
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em irreflexive graphs
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mo ⇉
                          %mo ⋅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\cdot\rightrightarrows\cdot}
                and the topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mrow
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mn 1
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\Delta_1^{op}}
                of
                %em reflexive graphs
                is a paradigmatic example of the distinction between a
                %em petit
                and a
                %em gros
                topos.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%B3nsson-Tarski+topos"} Jónsson-Tarski topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_2
                is an étendue, as it is locally equivalent to the sheaf topos on the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
                %em labeled graphs
                in (Lawvere 1989).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Proposition
                \. A Grothendieck topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒴
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
                is an étendue iff there exists a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, J)
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒴
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Y}
                such that every morphism of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                is monic.
            %li
              %p
                In particular for small
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝒞
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\mathcal{C}^{op}}
                is an étendue iff all morphisms in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                are monic. In particular for
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                this is sometimes called
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+cancellative+category"} left cancellative
                (e.g. each free monoid is left cancellative).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Subtoposes"} Subtoposes
                of étendues are étendues. K. Rosenthal uses this together with the preceding remark on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝒞
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^{\mathcal{C}^{op}}
                for all-monic
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                in order to construct further étendues
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Sh
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒮
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝒞
                            %mi op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_j(\mathcal{S}^{\mathcal{C}^{op}})
                via a topology
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
                from a suitable functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒮
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{S}
                (for further details see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%B3nsson-Tarski+topos"} Jónsson-Tarski topos
                or Rosenthal(1981)).
            %li
              %p
                A Grothendieck topos is a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+topos"} Boolean
                  étendue
                precisely if it satisfies the
                %em
                  internal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                (Freyd&amp;Scedrov 1990). An example of such a Boolean étendue is
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝒮
                          %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}^G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                a group.
            %li
              %p
                Étendues are ‘locally co-decidable’ in the sense that for a small
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                the functor category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{C},Set]
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+decidable+topos"} locally decidable topos
                precisely if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{C}^{op},Set]
                is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tendue"} étendue
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotents"} idempotents
                (McLarty 2006, Lawvere 2007).
          %h2#slice_toposes_of_tendues_are_tendues Slice toposes of étendues are étendues
          %p
            One can use the site characterization to show that being an étendue topos is a
            = succeed "." do
              %em local property
          #etendue_slices.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathcal{C},J)
              be an étendue topos with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, J)
              being an all-monic-site presentation and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P:C^{op}\to Set
              be a presheaf on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              that is a
              = succeed "-sheaf." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathcal{C},J)/P
              is an étendue topos.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              It suffices to show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathcal{C},J)/P
              has an all-monic site presentation. By exercise III.8 in
              %a{:href => "#MacLaneMoerdijk"} Mac Lane-Moerdijk (1994, p.157)
              there exists a topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J'
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_\mathcal{C} P
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi P
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathcal{C},J)/P\simeq Sh(\int_\mathcal{C} P,J')\; .
            %p
              Whence it suffices to show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_\mathcal{C} P
              is all-monic: Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:(C,x)\to (D,y)
              be a morphism in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_\mathcal{C} P
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g,h:(B,z)\rightrightarrows (C,x)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\cdot g=f\cdot h
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g=h
              since composition in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_\mathcal{C} P
              is inherited from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              and the latter was all-monic by assumption.
          #IAC_local.num_prop
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be a Grothendieck topos satisfying the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} internal axiom of choice
              (IAC). Then any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}/X
              satisfies the internal axiom of choice as well.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              By Freyd-Scedrov (
              %a{:href => "#FreydScedrov90"}> 1990
              , p.181) a Grothendieck topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              satisfies IAC iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              is a Boolean étendue. But being
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+category"} Boolean
              is a local property whence by the preceding proposition all slices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}/X
              are Boolean étendues.
